1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,400
Everything from a house to an eggshell has its point of failure.

2
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:20,740
When we study that instant of destruction, the lessons we learn are invaluable.

3
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:26,340
Because knowing can spell the difference between life and death.

4
00:00:26,341 --> 00:00:31,340
Now, Breaking Point on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:50,340
You can't truly know a thing until you see how strong it is.

6
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:54,340
That means you have to break it.

7
00:00:56,340 --> 00:01:04,440
Only then can you perceive its flaws, recognize its dangers, and learn if it will serve you

8
00:01:04,441 --> 00:01:09,120
or kill you.

9
00:01:09,121 --> 00:01:14,980
Materials Engineering Labs test the things we use every day.

10
00:01:14,981 --> 00:01:25,700
They blow them up, stress them, and push them until they can't take it anymore.

11
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:31,980
Some of the most dramatic results involve the vehicles we depend on.

12
00:01:31,981 --> 00:01:36,940
The Insurance Institute for Highway Safety, funded by automotive insurance companies,

13
00:01:36,941 --> 00:01:44,820
documents hundreds of tests each year.

14
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:52,700
Since 1969, the organization has researched road safety, studying cars, the people who

15
00:01:52,701 --> 00:02:01,580
drive them and the environments in which they're driven.

16
00:02:01,581 --> 00:02:05,980
Specialists use high-speed cameras to capture every millisecond of a crash in ultra-slow

17
00:02:05,981 --> 00:02:16,220
motion, providing a detailed look at multiple points of failure.

18
00:02:16,221 --> 00:02:22,220
In a crash test, an alternative propulsion system replaces the internal combustion engine.

19
00:02:22,221 --> 00:02:25,720
This allows for the velocity of the vehicle to be precisely controlled.

20
00:02:25,721 --> 00:02:31,220
We've got a hydraulic propulsion system that's going to pull this vehicle down the

21
00:02:31,221 --> 00:02:35,840
runway at 40 miles an hour and into that barrier behind us.

22
00:02:35,841 --> 00:02:44,260
A crash test dummy stands in for the living occupant of the car.

23
00:02:44,261 --> 00:02:47,440
Humans have their braking points too.

24
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:51,720
The institute has surrogates for every kind of driver and passenger.

25
00:02:51,721 --> 00:02:58,340
We have dummies ranging from a six-month-old baby to a 95th percentile male dummy, and

26
00:02:58,341 --> 00:03:03,040
that's covering a span from 17 pounds up to 223 pounds.

27
00:03:03,041 --> 00:03:06,760
A technician applies grease to the dummy's head.

28
00:03:06,761 --> 00:03:13,760
Upon impact, the grease leaves a mark on the airbag and interior of the vehicle, showing

29
00:03:13,761 --> 00:03:17,320
the points of contact.

30
00:03:17,321 --> 00:03:21,860
Immediately after the impact, data from the crash will be sent wirelessly to the Institute's

31
00:03:21,861 --> 00:03:24,140
computers for analysis.

32
00:03:24,141 --> 00:03:29,360
Those measurements get related back to human tolerance and that's how we evaluate how well

33
00:03:29,361 --> 00:03:31,340
a vehicle does in a crash test.

34
00:03:31,341 --> 00:03:35,740
In the chest there's some ribs and we can also measure how much the rib cage gets pushed

35
00:03:35,741 --> 00:03:41,500
either by the steering wheel or the seat belt.

36
00:03:41,501 --> 00:03:45,020
Down here in the legs we can tell how much force comes up through the legs, through the

37
00:03:45,021 --> 00:03:49,200
tibia, through the knee, and into the femur.

38
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:53,440
The dummies tell the story of the human breaking point, and they help to find the vehicle's

39
00:03:53,441 --> 00:03:56,760
breaking point as well.

40
00:03:56,761 --> 00:04:01,560
For the Insurance Institute, that isn't the point at which the metal crumples.

41
00:04:01,561 --> 00:04:08,120
It's the point at which the vehicle fails to protect its occupants in a crash.

42
00:04:08,121 --> 00:04:14,480
In the 1970s, the Institute demonstrated the need for lap safety belts.

43
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:19,480
The breaking point of a vehicle during crash tests was 35 miles per hour.

44
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:25,480
The 1980s brought airbags, and the point at which occupant compartment integrity should

45
00:04:25,481 --> 00:04:35,240
be maintained went up to 40 miles per hour in most vehicles.

46
00:04:35,241 --> 00:04:41,540
By 2008, safety technology combined lap and shoulder belts, additional airbags, and car

47
00:04:41,541 --> 00:04:45,000
Our body is designed to absorb impact.

48
00:04:45,001 --> 00:04:49,660
Let's see what happens in a 40 mile per hour crash of a recent model.

49
00:04:49,661 --> 00:04:54,720
What we'd like to see is the occupant compartment in front of the driver be well protected so

50
00:04:54,721 --> 00:04:58,140
that there's very little intrusion pushing in on the occupant and that keeps the injury

51
00:04:58,141 --> 00:04:59,140
risk very low.

52
00:04:59,141 --> 00:05:04,140
3, 2, 1.

53
00:05:04,141 --> 00:05:09,140
We're ready.

54
00:05:09,141 --> 00:05:17,620
The analysis begins moments after the crash.

55
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:24,120
Lower tibia index should indicate elevated risk of lower extremity fracture.

56
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:29,460
You can see that one side of the car here has been pushed in,

57
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:34,400
and in the offset test, it means that only about half of the car has to take all of the load.

58
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:38,400
That's the kind of crash that would happen if you're driving down a two-lane highway

59
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:40,300
and somebody crosses the center line a little bit.

60
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:46,200
This car looks, in this severe test, pretty good.

61
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,040
We've not catastrophically collapsed this car.

62
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:53,680
And that's something we saw with a lot of older designs

63
00:05:53,681 --> 00:05:56,960
where the vehicles weren't designed to be able to take the load

64
00:05:56,961 --> 00:05:58,040
in this offset configuration,

65
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:01,420
and we'd see massive intrusion into the occupant space

66
00:06:01,421 --> 00:06:03,340
so that there's no room at all for the dummy,

67
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:05,700
and in some cases, even having the steering wheel

68
00:06:05,701 --> 00:06:07,780
pushing directly on the dummy's chest.

69
00:06:07,781 --> 00:06:11,920
that's not good for occupant protection this vehicle held up pretty well and

70
00:06:11,921 --> 00:06:17,260
didn't hit the braking point the Institute also performs side angle

71
00:06:17,261 --> 00:06:23,400
crash tests using a hydraulically powered barrier

72
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:31,280
with no engine compartment buffering the occupant from the oncoming barrier the

73
00:06:31,281 --> 00:06:36,280
the impact can be severe.

74
00:06:36,281 --> 00:06:41,160
Side airbags have helped reduce injuries in this kind of crash.

75
00:06:41,161 --> 00:06:46,200
And according to the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety, so is sturdier reinforced

76
00:06:46,201 --> 00:06:52,780
construction built into the sides of many newer model vehicles.

77
00:06:52,781 --> 00:07:00,660
But there's another dimension to the breaking point of a car beyond what happens in a collision.

78
00:07:00,661 --> 00:07:07,660
Each year, thousands of people are injured when car batteries explode or catch fire.

79
00:07:07,661 --> 00:07:12,660
Battery safety is more important than ever since we're relying more and more on batteries

80
00:07:12,661 --> 00:07:19,660
to power the hybrids and electric cars of the future.

81
00:07:19,661 --> 00:07:27,660
That's a big reason why Sandia Laboratories in New Mexico is putting them to the test.

82
00:07:27,661 --> 00:07:36,840
For more than five decades, Sandia Labs has tested everything from atom bombs and underground

83
00:07:36,841 --> 00:07:40,480
explosions to spent nuclear fuel containers.

84
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:49,320
In 1977, in a test for the Department of Energy, Sandia crashed a truck carrying a 22-ton spent

85
00:07:49,321 --> 00:07:52,700
nuclear fuel cask at 60 miles per hour.

86
00:07:57,660 --> 00:08:00,280
They tried it at 84 miles per hour.

87
00:08:04,460 --> 00:08:09,400
In both tests, according to Sandia, the nuclear container did not reach its breaking point.

88
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:17,460
They hit a 25-ton shipping cask with a locomotive going 81 miles per hour.

89
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:26,060
A 74-ton nuclear shipping container crashing at 81 miles per hour.

90
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:29,420
No problem.

91
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:36,420
In 1988, Sandia created a test at the request of a Japanese company

92
00:08:36,421 --> 00:08:40,940
to determine the impact force of an F-4 Phantom Jack on a concrete target.

93
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,840
The wall was suspended on a cushion of compressed air

94
00:08:47,841 --> 00:08:50,400
so that the impact could be precisely measured.

95
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:55,620
At a speed of 480 miles per hour,

96
00:08:55,621 --> 00:08:57,860
The 12-foot-thick wall survived.

97
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:03,340
But the jet didn't fare as well.

98
00:09:08,420 --> 00:09:11,880
In Sandia's battery tests, the explosions may be smaller,

99
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:14,880
but the ramifications are just as far-reaching.

100
00:09:15,580 --> 00:09:18,700
The major concern that we have with lithium-ion systems

101
00:09:18,701 --> 00:09:23,520
that are applied in hybrid cars, plug-in hybrids,

102
00:09:23,521 --> 00:09:29,840
power assist hybrids is the fact that you're now talking about many cells in series, 40

103
00:09:29,841 --> 00:09:34,975
to 80 cells, pack voltages that could be at 300 volts or higher.

104
00:09:34,976 --> 00:09:36,660
We're looking at some

105
00:09:36,661 --> 00:09:42,560
of the more energetic materials and testing cells under worst possible conditions.

106
00:09:42,561 --> 00:09:48,600
When those cells reach their breaking point, either from overcharging or temperature extremes,

107
00:09:48,601 --> 00:09:54,360
They can explode and release toxic gases.

108
00:09:54,361 --> 00:09:59,480
Therefore the test environment is monitored by video and data collected remotely.

109
00:09:59,481 --> 00:10:05,560
Let's go ahead and start the overcharge, 2 amps, 20 volts.

110
00:10:05,561 --> 00:10:10,280
This overcharge test involves a cell created in the lab with a deliberate defect.

111
00:10:10,281 --> 00:10:16,300
It has metal particles at the core of the cell.

112
00:10:16,301 --> 00:10:19,600
these particles may compromise the separator.

113
00:10:19,601 --> 00:10:22,040
The separator is a divider within the cell,

114
00:10:22,041 --> 00:10:25,520
intended to keep the positive and negative electrodes apart

115
00:10:25,521 --> 00:10:28,180
and to prevent internal short circuits.

116
00:10:28,181 --> 00:10:31,640
When the separator fails, the battery shorts out.

117
00:10:31,641 --> 00:10:33,960
We're up to 4.5 volts.

118
00:10:33,961 --> 00:10:35,380
Are we going to see a heating rate change

119
00:10:35,381 --> 00:10:37,660
as we start getting closer to 5 volts?

120
00:10:37,661 --> 00:10:40,500
After it goes over the peak and starts coming back down,

121
00:10:40,501 --> 00:10:43,420
you will see a heating rate change.

122
00:10:43,421 --> 00:10:45,760
The team has put a spark source nearby

123
00:10:45,761 --> 00:10:48,760
to simulate sparks that might be present in an automobile accident.

124
00:10:48,761 --> 00:10:52,760
We're getting electrolyte breakdown. I would turn on the sparks now.

125
00:10:52,761 --> 00:10:57,760
There we go. We're approaching 135.

126
00:10:57,761 --> 00:11:04,760
Perfect. That was one of our best ones yet.

127
00:11:04,761 --> 00:11:11,760
The breaking point of this lithium-ion cell was 275 degrees Fahrenheit.

128
00:11:11,761 --> 00:11:12,461
Dr.

129
00:11:12,462 --> 00:11:17,120
Roth's experiments in the lab will help battery manufacturers determine the breaking

130
00:11:17,121 --> 00:11:27,000
point for their products, leading to safer, greener transportation in the future.

131
00:11:27,001 --> 00:11:32,360
Nearly 500 miles from Sandia Labs at Arizona State University, scientists have been making

132
00:11:32,361 --> 00:11:38,360
materials fail for more than 50 years.

133
00:11:38,361 --> 00:11:41,520
Dr. Barzin Moubashar runs the materials lab at ASU.

134
00:11:42,940 --> 00:11:45,500
The lab uses a Phantom 7 high-speed camera

135
00:11:45,501 --> 00:11:46,960
to record breaking points.

136
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:56,080
It's capable of shooting 30,000 frames a second.

137
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:00,960
The greater the number of images a camera can capture,

138
00:12:00,961 --> 00:12:02,780
the more the action can be slowed down.

139
00:12:02,781 --> 00:12:07,172
This extreme slow motion effect permits Dr.

140
00:12:07,173 --> 00:12:11,360
Mabasher to examine the breaking point in

141
00:12:11,361 --> 00:12:12,360
detail.

142
00:12:12,361 --> 00:12:20,820
Basically what we do is to try to characterize how materials perform under load that's applied

143
00:12:20,821 --> 00:12:21,820
to them.

144
00:12:21,821 --> 00:12:25,100
What makes certain materials different than the other ones?

145
00:12:25,101 --> 00:12:31,380
Paper versus wood versus steel or fibers, carbon fiber, Tevlon.

146
00:12:31,381 --> 00:12:36,820
In order to characterize the materials in that way, we do simple fundamental material

147
00:12:36,821 --> 00:12:39,960
properties tests.

148
00:12:39,961 --> 00:12:45,060
In this tension test, a hydraulic piston moves up or down to stress a specimen a few inches

149
00:12:45,061 --> 00:12:48,920
long.

150
00:12:48,921 --> 00:12:53,280
The lab is testing Kevlar for use in jet engine containment.

151
00:12:53,281 --> 00:12:59,440
Jet engine containment systems encase the entire housing of the fan blades inside the

152
00:12:59,441 --> 00:13:06,440
jet engine. In the unlikely event that we would have a failure of a fan blade, that

153
00:13:06,441 --> 00:13:10,685
casing material should act as a reverse bulletproof vest.

154
00:13:10,686 --> 00:13:13,440
It should contain the damage into the

155
00:13:13,441 --> 00:13:16,440
engine.

156
00:13:16,441 --> 00:13:21,440
Researchers place the sample in a specialized rig to align it for the high-speed camera

157
00:13:21,441 --> 00:13:24,440
and allow for accurate measurements from a load cell.

158
00:13:24,441 --> 00:13:33,000
3, 2, 1, fire.

159
00:13:33,001 --> 00:13:36,880
The breaking point of a 2 by 1 half inch strip of woven Kevlar?

160
00:13:36,881 --> 00:13:40,940
420 pounds of pulling force.

161
00:13:40,941 --> 00:13:48,560
Tests like these help engineers who put jet engines into extreme failure mode.

162
00:13:48,561 --> 00:13:53,300
As predicted by the results of materials lab testing, the Kevlar proved strong enough to

163
00:13:53,301 --> 00:13:58,580
contain a runaway turbine fan blade.

164
00:13:58,581 --> 00:14:02,780
Good thing someone studies the breaking point of a jet engine.

165
00:14:02,781 --> 00:14:06,360
But what about your own possession?

166
00:14:06,361 --> 00:14:10,140
Knowing the breaking point of a safe helps keep your stuff

167
00:14:10,141 --> 00:14:10,841
yours.

168
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:16,860
The most expensive cars ever crash tested by the Insurance

169
00:14:16,861 --> 00:14:21,140
Institute for Highway Safety are the Mercedes E500 and the

