It would be wide enough to get valuables out of that safe.

237
00:19:44,041 --> 00:19:48,240
The safe broke in less than 30 minutes.

238
00:19:48,241 --> 00:19:53,840
Jewelry would not be affected by this high heat, but paper money would burn up.

239
00:19:53,841 --> 00:19:58,540
It's bad news for the safe manufacturer, since UL reached the breaking point of this safe

240
00:19:58,541 --> 00:20:02,140
in under the time expected.

241
00:20:02,141 --> 00:20:06,540
What about the breaking points of the tools in your garage?

242
00:20:06,541 --> 00:20:09,480
Are they what they should be?

243
00:20:09,481 --> 00:20:15,640
Stanley Tools in Connecticut has been in business for more than 100 years.

244
00:20:15,641 --> 00:20:22,540
For at least the last 50, they've been putting their products to the test in the lab.

245
00:20:22,541 --> 00:20:32,300
They pull the heads off hammers, stress out tape measures, and love to drop things.

246
00:20:32,301 --> 00:20:37,140
The hammer slammer test, as Stanley technicians affectionately call it, is actually designed

247
00:20:37,141 --> 00:20:40,040
to help the carpenter with poor aim.

248
00:20:40,041 --> 00:20:48,860
It's going to strike this hammer on the handle and not on the striking face of the hammer

249
00:20:48,861 --> 00:20:51,300
to simulate someone missing the product

250
00:20:51,301 --> 00:20:53,380
that they're trying to hit with the hammer,

251
00:20:53,381 --> 00:20:54,960
and they actually hit the handle.

252
00:20:59,160 --> 00:21:01,460
That's a Stanley hammer and a competitor's

253
00:21:01,461 --> 00:21:03,280
in the test rig going head to head.

254
00:21:06,960 --> 00:21:09,260
The beat goes on for about 40 blows.

255
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:14,360
And then, failure.

256
00:21:14,361 --> 00:21:21,360
The competitor's hammer loses its head first.

257
00:21:21,361 --> 00:21:29,360
But the slamming continues until...

258
00:21:29,361 --> 00:21:36,360
The Stanley hammer also fails.

259
00:21:36,361 --> 00:21:43,360
also fails.

260
00:21:43,361 --> 00:21:50,660
Stanley has made the most of the demonstration, even making a TV commercial featuring it.

261
00:21:50,661 --> 00:21:56,340
Destructive tests like this can lead to improved tools.

262
00:21:56,341 --> 00:22:01,780
Before hammers were designed without the polycarbonate on them, they just had the fiberglass core.

263
00:22:01,781 --> 00:22:06,880
What would happen is when someone would over strike on the hammer, the fiberglass would

264
00:22:06,881 --> 00:22:09,460
fray and then get into people's hands.

265
00:22:09,461 --> 00:22:16,140
By putting the polycarbonate covering around it, that stopped the fraying.

266
00:22:16,141 --> 00:22:20,960
Stanley acknowledges the fact that some of us use our tools for unintended purposes.

267
00:22:20,961 --> 00:22:30,360
In the field, people use levels incorrectly, using it to carry things, using it to pry.

268
00:22:30,361 --> 00:22:34,920
The three-point test attacks the weakest point of the level, the vial.

269
00:22:34,921 --> 00:22:41,040
Mounted in a test rig, the pressure builds and the level eventually bends.

270
00:22:41,041 --> 00:22:44,800
What it would do is stretch and then start to tear.

271
00:22:44,801 --> 00:22:50,280
What we do not want on a level is a catastrophic failure which would be a snap because that

272
00:22:50,281 --> 00:22:52,160
would be bad for the end user.

273
00:22:52,161 --> 00:22:57,960
This gives the end user ample warning that the level is breaking and tearing before they

274
00:22:57,961 --> 00:22:58,960
would get injured.

275
00:23:00,360 --> 00:23:04,440
Taking a product to its breaking point can teach technicians a lot about how it behaves.

276
00:23:07,960 --> 00:23:09,885
How strong is your bike lock?

277
00:23:09,886 --> 00:23:13,800
Arizona State University's Materials Lab wants to find out.

278
00:23:14,440 --> 00:23:20,120
We're going to do a tension test on this bicycle lock and we'll be grabbing it using a couple of

279
00:23:20,121 --> 00:23:24,280
grips and then we're going to be pulling down upon it so that it'll actually be pulling apart

280
00:23:24,281 --> 00:23:28,120
around the mechanism here where it's just locking into place.

281
00:23:28,121 --> 00:23:32,680
Five, four, three, two, one.

282
00:23:36,200 --> 00:23:39,400
500 pounds of upward force.

283
00:23:39,401 --> 00:23:41,480
1,000.

284
00:23:41,481 --> 00:23:44,520
2,000.

285
00:23:44,521 --> 00:23:48,040
The lock holds, but you can hear the clicks of strain.

286
00:23:54,280 --> 00:24:00,040
The breaking point of this bike lock is 5,121 pounds of upward force.

287
00:24:00,860 --> 00:24:05,380
I would have expected the locking mechanism, truthfully, to fail.

288
00:24:05,640 --> 00:24:11,640
But the locking mechanism was so strong that basically we have just a gross section of the material failing.

289
00:24:13,040 --> 00:24:15,920
The breaking point of a lock can cost you your possessions.

290
00:24:18,580 --> 00:24:24,020
But when a bridge or a building abruptly fails, you can lose immeasurably more.

291
00:24:24,280 --> 00:24:33,240
Since children could be trapped inside refrigerators if their doors reach the breaking point,

292
00:24:33,980 --> 00:24:39,620
Underwriters Laboratories opens and closes test models 300,000 times.

293
00:24:40,980 --> 00:24:44,180
Breaking Point will return on Modern Marvels.

294
00:24:47,800 --> 00:24:49,240
Steel and concrete.

295
00:24:50,320 --> 00:24:52,800
These are the materials of the buildings we inhabit

296
00:24:52,801 --> 00:24:54,500
and the roads we travel.

297
00:24:56,040 --> 00:24:58,020
When they reach their breaking points,

298
00:24:58,260 --> 00:24:59,740
the results can be catastrophic.

299
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:05,680
In 1940, the Tacoma Narrows Bridge

300
00:25:05,681 --> 00:25:07,000
was an engineering innovation

301
00:25:07,001 --> 00:25:10,100
spanning 2,800 feet between Tacoma

302
00:25:10,101 --> 00:25:12,720
and the Kitschop Peninsula in Washington State.

303
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:17,140
Its designers assumed that its cables

304
00:25:17,141 --> 00:25:18,800
would absorb half the wind energy

305
00:25:18,801 --> 00:25:20,140
hitting the bridge from the side.

306
00:25:20,141 --> 00:25:23,140
They were wrong.

307
00:25:24,140 --> 00:25:27,140
At 11 a.m. on November 7, 1940,

308
00:25:28,140 --> 00:25:31,900
winds of only about 40 miles per hour generated an unstable oscillation,

309
00:25:32,700 --> 00:25:34,180
known as aeroelastic flutter.

310
00:25:35,820 --> 00:25:39,660
When the left side of the bridge went up, the right side went down,

311
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:42,880
with the oscillations increasing in amplitude with each cycle.

312
00:25:45,400 --> 00:25:47,600
Finally, the bridge reached its breaking point.

313
00:25:50,140 --> 00:26:01,020
A breaking point in Missouri caused the deaths of 114 people and injured 200 more.

314
00:26:01,021 --> 00:26:09,460
On July 17, 1981, the Kansas City Hyatt Regency Hotel was the site of a dance contest.

315
00:26:09,461 --> 00:26:14,900
Thousands of people gathered to watch from walkways suspended over the lobby.

316
00:26:14,901 --> 00:26:19,580
Connections supporting the rods holding the walkways failed.

317
00:26:19,581 --> 00:26:21,580
The breaking point proved fatal.

318
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:28,020
The World Trade Towers met their tragic breaking point

319
00:26:28,021 --> 00:26:31,700
on 9-11 when terrorists flew two aircraft into them.

320
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,140
The powerful impact of the aircraft blew off

321
00:26:36,141 --> 00:26:39,540
fireproofing insulation that was protecting steel supports

322
00:26:39,541 --> 00:26:40,840
inside the towers.

323
00:26:42,340 --> 00:26:44,280
The heat of the fires weakened the steel.

324
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:47,500
It bent and failed.

325
00:26:49,580 --> 00:27:02,580
Engineers study these disasters in order to design better structures.

326
00:27:02,581 --> 00:27:07,320
Sometimes they perform destructive tests, like loading bridges with concrete blocks

327
00:27:07,321 --> 00:27:12,300
until they reach the breaking point.

328
00:27:12,301 --> 00:27:16,900
Mostly engineers break materials in the lab

329
00:27:16,901 --> 00:27:21,120
and use the data to create complex models.

330
00:27:21,121 --> 00:27:23,780
When we measure material properties,

331
00:27:23,781 --> 00:27:25,740
we apply a certain amount of load

332
00:27:25,741 --> 00:27:30,420
and we see how it deforms, how it fails.

333
00:27:30,421 --> 00:27:34,040
We use this information in order to do modeling.

334
00:27:34,041 --> 00:27:38,360
And in modeling work, we try to explain a complex structure,

335
00:27:38,361 --> 00:27:39,860
such as a bridge.

336
00:27:39,861 --> 00:27:42,620
We can design a structure before it is built,

337
00:27:42,621 --> 00:27:44,700
or predict the strength of a structure

338
00:27:44,701 --> 00:27:46,800
if we know what the properties are.

339
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:50,240
They've learned that a large structure's failure

340
00:27:50,241 --> 00:27:52,400
can begin with a microcrack,

341
00:27:52,401 --> 00:27:54,840
a fissure 10 times thinner than a human hair.

342
00:27:56,300 --> 00:27:59,180
These are probably of the order of a micron in size.

343
00:27:59,181 --> 00:28:00,980
As the load is slowly increased,

344
00:28:00,981 --> 00:28:04,120
these microcracks start growing.

345
00:28:04,121 --> 00:28:08,420
They eventually find one another or affect one another.

346
00:28:08,421 --> 00:28:11,020
And as the micro cracks grow, they may coalesce,

347
00:28:11,021 --> 00:28:14,340
they may reach to one another and become larger and larger,

348
00:28:14,341 --> 00:28:17,180
until we reach that criteria that now you have

349
00:28:17,181 --> 00:28:18,600
a large enough of a crack.

350
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:22,400
To witness that up close,

351
00:28:22,401 --> 00:28:24,560
scientists use a microforce tester

352
00:28:24,561 --> 00:28:27,600
to fracture specimens as thin as four hundredths of an inch.

353
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:32,580
This machine is particularly suited for very small loads

354
00:28:32,581 --> 00:28:34,320
and very small displacements.

355
00:28:34,321 --> 00:28:36,540
Ceramic fibers and the carbon fibers

356
00:28:36,541 --> 00:28:39,460
are filaments that are thinner than a human hair.

357
00:28:42,720 --> 00:28:46,200
Starting with a micron and scaling up to a skyscraper

358
00:28:46,201 --> 00:28:49,560
involves advanced calculation.

359
00:28:49,561 --> 00:28:53,680
But it might someday save countless lives.

360
00:28:53,681 --> 00:28:56,700
While steel and concrete may be some of the strongest materials

361
00:28:56,701 --> 00:28:59,760
we use on massive construction projects,

362
00:28:59,761 --> 00:29:00,740
they are vulnerable.

363
00:29:00,741 --> 00:29:11,600
On January 17, 1994, a magnitude 6.7 earthquake struck the city of Los Angeles, centered in

364
00:29:11,601 --> 00:29:14,640
the area called Northridge.

365
00:29:14,641 --> 00:29:22,400
There was more than $40 billion in damage, and at least 57 people died.

366
00:29:22,401 --> 00:29:27,540
Researchers wanted to know why some buildings withstood the assault, while others failed.

367
00:29:27,541 --> 00:29:34,540
They turned to a device called a shake table.

368
00:29:34,541 --> 00:29:44,540
Using data gathered from sensors in the field, the shake table can replay the destructive power of a quake.

369
00:29:44,541 --> 00:29:53,540
Scientists were surprised to discover that the breaking point came when brittle steel connections between concrete beams and concrete columns failed.

370
00:29:53,541 --> 00:29:57,580
Steel connections also failed between concrete slabs in parking structures.

371
00:29:59,140 --> 00:30:05,780
Out of the lessons learned from these breaking point studies came new building codes to make constructions safer.

372
00:30:07,560 --> 00:30:11,760
Like concrete and steel, the human body has its breaking points.

373
00:30:12,900 --> 00:30:16,700
But experts are vigilantly trying to prevent us from reaching them.

374
00:30:18,100 --> 00:30:22,520
In 1989, engineers determined the breaking point of the Tower of Pisa.

375
00:30:22,521 --> 00:30:26,240
It would collapse when its tilt reached 5.4 degrees.

376
00:30:26,880 --> 00:30:30,380
But inexplicably, it was already leaning 5.5 degrees.

377
00:30:31,120 --> 00:30:34,480
A $25 million project set it a little straighter.

378
00:30:35,220 --> 00:30:37,680
Breaking Point will return on Modern Marvels.

379
00:30:40,680 --> 00:30:45,720
Built to last close to 100 years, the human body can take a lot of punishment.

380
00:30:47,440 --> 00:30:49,360
But it has its breaking points.

381
00:30:50,200 --> 00:30:51,800
Witness the human skull.

382
00:30:52,520 --> 00:30:57,420
protector of the vital control system we call the brain.

383
00:30:57,421 --> 00:31:01,940
A force generating 900 PSI can fracture the frontal bone,

384
00:31:01,941 --> 00:31:05,420
the equivalent of a seven pound, one inch square hammerhead

385
00:31:05,421 --> 00:31:10,300
contacting the skull at 13 miles per hour.

386
00:31:10,301 --> 00:31:11,920
The breaking point of the side of the skull

387
00:31:11,921 --> 00:31:16,400
is half that much, 450 PSI, easily reached

388
00:31:16,401 --> 00:31:18,180
by falling from a slow moving bike

389
00:31:18,181 --> 00:31:19,480
and hitting a sidewalk curb.

390
00:31:22,520 --> 00:31:29,520
Helmets are evaluated by performing tests that simulate an impact with the ground.

391
00:31:29,521 --> 00:31:34,520
The test measures the amount of G's applied to the rider's head during an impact.

392
00:31:34,521 --> 00:31:36,486
A G is one Earth gravity.

393
00:31:36,487 --> 00:31:41,520
G's are units of measurement used to quantify force on the body.

394
00:31:41,521 --> 00:31:48,520
According to the Bicycle Helmet Safety Institute, if a rider fell from a bike without a helmet onto a hard surface,

395
00:31:48,521 --> 00:31:51,600
his brain would experience at least 1,000 Gs.

396
00:31:52,200 --> 00:31:54,360
The brain would move violently in the skull,

397
00:31:54,780 --> 00:31:57,300
rupturing blood vessels and probably causing a concussion.

398
00:32:00,380 --> 00:32:02,080
That's why the skull needs help.

399
00:32:03,760 --> 00:32:06,900
Researchers have found that keeping G-forces below 300

400
00:32:06,901 --> 00:32:09,960
provides adequate protection for the helmeted head.

401
00:32:14,060 --> 00:32:16,980
The breaking point of the orbital bone that holds the eye

402
00:32:16,981 --> 00:32:19,200
is just two foot pounds of energy.

403
00:32:20,640 --> 00:32:22,940
Getting hit in the eye with a racquetball in normal play

404
00:32:22,941 --> 00:32:23,960
would easily do it.

405
00:32:25,180 --> 00:32:28,540
At Oakley, they're serious about protecting the organ of sight.

406
00:32:29,820 --> 00:32:32,080
They design and manufacture eyeglasses

407
00:32:32,081 --> 00:32:33,460
and goggles for the military.

408
00:32:38,820 --> 00:32:40,960
They also make eye protection built

409
00:32:40,961 --> 00:32:43,420
to take the toughest abuse athletes can dish out.

410
00:32:43,421 --> 00:32:51,280
One item technicians tested Oakley's R&D lab is the eye guard on a football helmet.

411
00:32:54,220 --> 00:32:57,720
Can it take a hockey puck going 80 miles per hour?

412
00:33:00,400 --> 00:33:01,420
No problem.

413
00:33:03,940 --> 00:33:06,660
Even everyday eyewear takes some serious abuse.

414
00:33:07,220 --> 00:33:10,200
This test is a high mass impact device.

415
00:33:10,201 --> 00:33:15,740
It was built based on the American National Standards Institute Z87.1 standard.

416
00:33:15,860 --> 00:33:17,420
It's an industrial standard for safety.

417
00:33:18,540 --> 00:33:23,420
Dropping a one-half-ounce steel ball from a distance of 54 inches is an easy test.

418
00:33:26,560 --> 00:33:29,500
But how about a one-pound conical-tipped missile?

419
00:33:31,580 --> 00:33:34,380
And you can see that basically that lens shattered.

420
00:33:34,480 --> 00:33:35,180
There's a big hole in it.

421
00:33:35,200 --> 00:33:39,020
So you'd have lens debris, frame debris, and that missile basically sticking in your eye

422
00:33:39,021 --> 00:33:39,980
if you were wearing this product.

423
00:33:40,200 --> 00:33:46,780
Mini glasses will fail this test unless they're made with a special hardened polycarbonate

424
00:33:46,781 --> 00:33:47,780
lens.

425
00:33:47,781 --> 00:33:53,240
You can see from that hit all you received was a slight scratch or indentation in the

426
00:33:53,241 --> 00:33:56,380
lens which means your eye was completely protected.

427
00:33:56,381 --> 00:34:01,620
Modern Marvels asked Oakley to take their eyewear past the breaking point.

428
00:34:01,621 --> 00:34:06,960
That meant moving on to the ballistics bench to test Oakley's SI assault goggles designed

429
00:34:06,961 --> 00:34:08,760
for military and law enforcement use.

430
00:34:10,640 --> 00:34:12,940
You have a variety of different fragments

431
00:34:12,941 --> 00:34:15,240
that could be from RPGs or what have you.

432
00:34:15,500 --> 00:34:18,280
It's critical to know where that lens's breaking point is

433
00:34:18,281 --> 00:34:20,440
so that way, not only for the soldier knowing

434
00:34:20,441 --> 00:34:21,720
what level of protection he has,

435
00:34:21,900 --> 00:34:23,640
but also that way the military knows

436
00:34:23,641 --> 00:34:26,300
what type of eyewear to prescribe to the soldier.

437
00:34:27,480 --> 00:34:30,540
Compressed nitrogen propels 22-caliber fragments

438
00:34:30,541 --> 00:34:33,040
to 375 miles per hour.

439
00:34:33,041 --> 00:34:41,420
we're using compressed nitrogen to propel at those speeds and you have to use that to get it that high

440
00:34:41,421 --> 00:34:44,380
you just can't use compressed air you can't get near the power behind it

441
00:34:44,381 --> 00:34:52,200
the 22 caliber frag damaged the lens but there was no penetration no breaking point yet

442
00:34:52,201 --> 00:34:56,340
the technician turns up the nitrogen pressure

443
00:34:56,341 --> 00:35:07,760
the 22 caliber fragment reached the speed of 511 miles per hour and took the lens past the

444
00:35:07,761 --> 00:35:13,800
breaking point basically you found a slight penetration in the lens right there frag

445
00:35:13,801 --> 00:35:20,020
actually is inside these goggles won't protect a soldier from a point blank 22 rifle round

446
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:23,280
That bullet goes over 600 miles per hour.

447
00:35:24,540 --> 00:35:27,920
But it can help him avoid shrapnel injuries from an IED

448
00:35:27,921 --> 00:35:31,340
or improvised explosive device.

449
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:37,180
Other parts of the body are tougher than the eye and can take more abuse.

450
00:35:38,700 --> 00:35:44,360
The tibia, a bone in the lower leg, breaks at 755 pounds of force in a male

451
00:35:44,361 --> 00:35:47,740
and about 500 pounds of force in a female

452
00:35:47,741 --> 00:35:52,580
if the force is applied near the middle of the bone and the foot is fixed in place.

453
00:35:55,400 --> 00:35:58,820
A football tackle from the side would easily generate that force

454
00:35:58,821 --> 00:36:01,700
if the player weren't able to move his leg out of the way.

455
00:36:02,280 --> 00:36:02,980
Set.

456
00:36:04,480 --> 00:36:05,180
Go.

457
00:36:07,380 --> 00:36:15,320
Hanger prosthetics and orthotics have studied athletes to help make knees, legs, arms, and hands.

458
00:36:15,321 --> 00:36:19,420
It's challenging to emulate human bones.

459
00:36:19,421 --> 00:36:21,620
Bone is very, very strong.

460
00:36:21,621 --> 00:36:23,120
It's very resilient.

461
00:36:23,121 --> 00:36:25,820
It'll actually adapt and change its shape,

462
00:36:25,821 --> 00:36:30,620
actually get thicker and harder in areas where it encounters more stress.

463
00:36:30,621 --> 00:36:34,320
The long bones of our lower extremities,

464
00:36:34,321 --> 00:36:37,220
they're designed to withstand tremendous amount of force

465
00:36:37,221 --> 00:36:39,320
before they reach their breaking point.

466
00:36:39,321 --> 00:36:41,720
Designers work with advanced materials

467
00:36:41,721 --> 00:36:44,220
to fashion devices as strong as human bones.

468
00:36:44,221 --> 00:36:51,360
We use carbon composite materials in many of the devices that we fabricate.

469
00:36:51,361 --> 00:36:54,820
Titanium is used because it's very, very strong, but it's also very light.

470
00:36:54,821 --> 00:37:02,340
At ASU's materials lab, a test performed for modern marvels revealed that a pylon,

471
00:37:02,341 --> 00:37:07,940
the device designed to emulate the tibia, could take the same amount of upward pulling

472
00:37:07,941 --> 00:37:11,780
force as a human tibia.

473
00:37:11,781 --> 00:37:17,940
meets the goal of creating a prosthetic that is as strong and as light as the amazing piece

474
00:37:17,941 --> 00:37:23,780
of biological engineering we call a leg bone.

475
00:37:23,781 --> 00:37:28,540
Knowing the point at which bones break may help sports equipment manufacturers, but if

476
00:37:28,541 --> 00:37:35,140
you're a climber, you might want to know the breaking point of this little piece of aluminum

477
00:37:35,141 --> 00:37:41,680
because your life may depend on it.

478
00:37:41,681 --> 00:37:45,200
The human eardrum can register a rock concert at 110 decibels.

479
00:37:45,980 --> 00:37:51,180
But at 160 decibels, it reaches its breaking point, perforating instantly.

480
00:37:52,720 --> 00:37:55,600
Breaking Point will return on Modern Marvels.

481
00:38:02,520 --> 00:38:06,960
Adventure sports push participants to the limit and sometimes past it.

482
00:38:07,700 --> 00:38:10,260
That's when the breaking point of their equipment becomes critical.

483
00:38:10,261 --> 00:38:12,660
Fabrics will simply leak.

484
00:38:13,660 --> 00:38:15,000
The wind will blow through them.

485
00:38:16,780 --> 00:38:17,880
They will tear.

486
00:38:18,260 --> 00:38:19,140
They will wear out.

487
00:38:19,780 --> 00:38:20,940
A sleeve will fall off.

488
00:38:21,520 --> 00:38:22,540
A hood won't fit.

489
00:38:23,240 --> 00:38:24,340
A zipper will fail.

490
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:29,040
In extreme condition, that's a really, really bad thing to have happen.

491
00:38:31,240 --> 00:38:35,520
Patagonia Clothing Company began making mountain climbing equipment in the 1950s.

492
00:38:35,521 --> 00:38:38,860
When it decided to move into high-performance clothing,

493
00:38:38,861 --> 00:38:42,400
Patagonia approached the task knowing the gear would be used

494
00:38:42,401 --> 00:38:44,200
in the most demanding environments on Earth.

495
00:38:44,201 --> 00:38:54,200
A simple thing like a zipper blowing apart in the middle of a climb in Alaska is huge

496
00:38:54,201 --> 00:38:58,340
because hypothermia and cold are very, very real situations.

497
00:38:58,341 --> 00:39:04,340
If you're on an extended boat trip and your waterproof fabric isn't being waterproof,

498
00:39:04,341 --> 00:39:06,900
then you're soaking wet.

499
00:39:06,901 --> 00:39:13,180
In Patagonia's R&D lab, that translates to jamming zippers with sand, or freezing them,

500
00:39:13,181 --> 00:39:19,020
and then running them through 2,000 cycles to see how they perform.

501
00:39:19,021 --> 00:39:23,000
It means stressing fabric to its breaking point.

502
00:39:23,001 --> 00:39:27,920
The tinsel tester has two cross heads where we attach the fabric, and the upper cross

503
00:39:27,921 --> 00:39:32,140
head is attached to a transducer that measures the breaking strength of the fabric as it

504
00:39:32,141 --> 00:39:33,140
pulls it apart.

505
00:39:33,141 --> 00:39:40,460
This one by four inch strip of fabric can handle 113 pounds of force.

506
00:39:40,461 --> 00:39:45,580
That means you could hang seven regulation weight bowling balls from it.

507
00:39:45,581 --> 00:39:49,460
A piece of fabric that strong might protect you on the summit.

508
00:39:49,461 --> 00:39:53,180
But what if you're in the vertical world on the way up?

509
00:39:53,181 --> 00:39:57,900
This is Little Cottonwood Canyon near Salt Lake City in Utah.

510
00:39:57,901 --> 00:40:00,860
fly on the wall is world-class climber Mike LeBecky.

511
00:40:03,040 --> 00:40:06,860
As I was getting up into what I would consider the really nice part of that climb,

512
00:40:07,380 --> 00:40:12,240
where the cracks are parallel, you know, it was a really good place to stage some falls.

513
00:40:13,480 --> 00:40:18,460
Mike tests gear for Black Diamond, a climbing equipment company founded in 1988.

514
00:40:19,980 --> 00:40:22,120
Today, he's focusing on cams.

515
00:40:22,121 --> 00:40:26,080
used with carabiners and connected with a belay line.

516
00:40:26,760 --> 00:40:29,100
Cams form a critical safety system for the climber.

517
00:40:29,720 --> 00:40:31,860
If the crack is very parallel sided,

518
00:40:32,700 --> 00:40:38,360
the force downward makes these cam lobes want to roll out against the crack.

519
00:40:38,361 --> 00:40:43,520
That rolling out, that expansion, creates greater force against the outside of the crack

520
00:40:43,521 --> 00:40:46,400
and then holds them securely in place.

521
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:50,940
You can see how these sort of barbed camming devices work.

522
00:40:50,941 --> 00:40:57,260
They actually go in the crack and it's, in my mind, it's almost mathematically impossible for them to pull out when you fall.

523
00:40:57,460 --> 00:41:04,460
And so staging those falls and using them in really nice cracks like that, it's pretty easy and it really shows how that stuff works.

524
00:41:05,800 --> 00:41:08,040
Unless a piece is stressed to its breaking point.

525
00:41:12,140 --> 00:41:18,500
Our gear, because it's got to be as light and as compact as possible, is ready to failure mode.

526
00:41:18,501 --> 00:41:22,140
So we have to absolutely ensure the integrity of our ratings

527
00:41:22,141 --> 00:41:24,840
to make sure that they perform the way we say they will

528
00:41:24,841 --> 00:41:26,340
so that people can count on it.

529
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:31,740
That's exactly what Black Diamond does every day in the lab.

530
00:41:33,340 --> 00:41:35,660
This is our dial-o-matic crack.

531
00:41:35,661 --> 00:41:38,760
You can see it's got granite plates on the inside.

532
00:41:38,761 --> 00:41:41,260
The cams are tested in real rock surface.

533
00:41:43,800 --> 00:41:46,800
The testing machine exerts greater and greater pull on the cam.

534
00:41:46,801 --> 00:41:51,800
a thousand,

535
00:41:51,801 --> 00:41:55,800
two thousand,

536
00:41:55,801 --> 00:42:02,800
and finally, the breaking point.

537
00:42:02,801 --> 00:42:05,800
In this test, we had the preferred failure mode.

538
00:42:05,801 --> 00:42:06,800
The cable broke.

539
00:42:06,801 --> 00:42:11,800
So if we can verify that the whole unit is at least as strong as the cable,

540
00:42:11,801 --> 00:42:13,800
then we've got a good unit.

541
00:42:13,801 --> 00:42:18,100
The peak value is 3,598 pounds.

542
00:42:18,101 --> 00:42:22,580
That's the amount of force required to cause the cam to fail.

543
00:42:25,080 --> 00:42:26,900
Carabiners get the same rough treatment,

544
00:42:28,280 --> 00:42:30,040
a repetitive test of the gait,

545
00:42:31,020 --> 00:42:34,420
and a brute force assessment of the static load they can handle.

546
00:42:37,380 --> 00:42:42,860
This neutrino carabiner is rated at about 5,395 pounds of hanging weight.

547
00:42:43,800 --> 00:42:45,800
Let's put it to the test.

548
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:53,800
You're seeing the live load as the crosshead moves and the carabiner gets stretched.

549
00:42:53,801 --> 00:42:56,800
5,000 pounds and then some.

550
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:01,800
The carabiner actually exceeds its rating.

551
00:43:01,801 --> 00:43:07,800
With a breaking point of nearly 6,000 pounds, you could hang a pickup truck from it.

552
00:43:07,801 --> 00:43:10,800
This equipment becomes my partner.

553
00:43:10,801 --> 00:43:14,160
These carabiners, the camming devices, everything you're putting in the cracks,

554
00:43:14,440 --> 00:43:17,060
you really can rely on them for thousands of pounds of weight.

555
00:43:17,540 --> 00:43:19,520
It's pretty unbelievable. It's amazing.

556
00:43:20,900 --> 00:43:25,200
For Mike and others like him, the equipment will only continue to get better.

557
00:43:25,840 --> 00:43:30,560
Because somewhere, someone is taking it to the breaking point.

